The problem asks us to simulate a process involving $N$ aliens over $N$ years.
Let $N$ be the number of aliens.
The $i$-th alien (1-indexed) starts with $A_i$ stones.
The $i$-th alien becomes an adult exactly $i$ years later.
This means:
- At year 1, the 1st alien becomes an adult.
- At year 2, the 2nd alien becomes an adult.
- ...
- At year $i$, the $i$-th alien becomes an adult.
- ...
- At year $N$, the $N$-th alien becomes an adult.

The rule for giving stones is:
"When someone becomes an adult on this planet, every adult who has at least one stone gives exactly one stone as a congratulatory gift to the alien who has just become an adult."

We need to find the number of stones each alien has after $N$ years.
Let $C_i(t)$ be the number of stones the $i$-th alien has at year $t$.
Initially at $t=0$, $C_i(0) = A_i$.

Let's analyze the events.
At year $i$, the $i$-th alien becomes an adult.
Who are the adults at year $i$?
The aliens who have already become adults are $1, 2, \ldots, i$.
So, at year $i$, the set of adults is $\{1, 2, \ldots, i\}$.
The $i$-th alien becomes an adult at this moment.
The rule says: "every adult who has at least one stone gives exactly one stone... to the alien who has just become an adult".
The "alien who has just become an adult" is the $i$-th alien.
So, at year $i$, the $i$-th alien receives a stone from every adult $j \in \{1, \ldots, i\}$ such that $C_j(i^-) \ge 1$, where $C_j(i^-)$ is the number of stones alien $j$ has just before the transfer at year $i$.
