We want to find $r_N$.

Since $N$ is up to $2 \times 10^5$ and $Q$ is up to $3 \times 10^5$, a direct simulation for each query would take $O(N \cdot Q)$ time, which is too slow ($6 \times 10^{10}$ operations). We need a more efficient approach.

Let's analyze the condition $L_i \le r_{i-1} \le R_i$.
Notice that the rating only increases. So $r_i \ge r_{i-1}$.
Specifically, $r_i = r_{i-1} + \mathbb{I}(L_i \le r_{i-1} \le R_i)$.
The final rating is $r_N = X + \sum_{i=1}^N \mathbb{I}(L_i \le r_{i-1} \le R_i)$.
The term $\mathbb{I}(L_i \le r_{i-1} \le R_i)$ depends on the current rating, which depends on $X$. This dependency makes it tricky.

